% ============================================================================
% precipitation-types — the common types of precipitation and the defining
% physical form each one is / reaches the ground as (adj-facts-stdlib,
% METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big ideas of weather watching, and a rung a FORECASTING agent
% reasons UP from: the water that falls out of the sky arrives in a handful of
% distinct FORMS, and the National Weather Service names each one by the shape of
% water it is. "Rain is liquid drops; snow is ice crystals; sleet reaches the
% ground as frozen rain drops; freezing rain is liquid that glazes to ice on
% contact; hail is balls of ice from a thunderstorm." Before you can reason about
% a winter storm, a road-ice warning, or a severe-weather report you must first
% know WHICH precipitation type you are looking at. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? precip_form(snow, $Form)      % ice_crystals
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `precip_form(precip, form)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the form
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a word that
% is not one of these precipitation types (it never invents a form).
%
% Each precipitation type and the defining physical form the source states for
% it, as a little truth table:
%
%     precipitation type   defining physical form   a beginner's cue
%     ------------------   ----------------------   -----------------------------
%     rain                 drops                    liquid water drops
%     snow                 ice_crystals             snowflakes are ice crystals
%     sleet                frozen_raindrops         ice pellets that bounce
%     freezing_rain        glaze_of_ice             rain that freezes on contact
%     hail                 balls_of_ice             from a thunderstorm cloud
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a form and recall its precipitation type:
%
%     ? precip_form($Precip, balls_of_ice)   % hail
%
% A word that is NOT one of these precipitation types — `fog`, `dew`, `mist` —
% is deliberately NOT a row, so a precipitation recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a form. (Fog is a suspension of droplets, not falling precipitation;
% it is left out on purpose.) That honest-abstention property is what makes the
% table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the type→form pairs it
% can ground, and only those.
%
% The `form` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be the
% defining physical form the source EXPLICITLY states for that type — never one
% the source does not support. Each atom uses the source's own word (the NWS says
% snowflakes are "ice crystals"; sleet reaches the ground as "frozen rain drops";
% freezing rain creates "a glaze of ice"; hail is "balls of ice"), so every value
% stays literally checkable against the quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every type→form pair below
% was read out of a fetched U.S. National Weather Service (NOAA) page, and any
% pair whose form could not be verified there would have been dropped). Each
% pair, with the primary source it was copied from and the verbatim span that
% states it:
%
%   rain → drops
%     https://forecast.weather.gov/glossary.php?word=RAIN
%     (NOAA National Weather Service Glossary — U.S. government)
%     "Precipitation that falls to earth in drops more than 0.5 mm in diameter."
%
%   snow → ice_crystals
%     https://www.weather.gov/iwx/sleetvsfreezingrain
%     (NOAA National Weather Service — U.S. government)
%     "Snowflakes are just collections of ice crystals that cling to each other
%      as they fall toward the ground."
%
%   sleet → frozen_raindrops
%     https://www.weather.gov/iwx/sleetvsfreezingrain
%     (NOAA National Weather Service — U.S. government)
%     "These slushy drops refreeze as they next fall through a deep layer of
%      freezing air above the surface, and eventually reach the ground as frozen
%      rain drops that bounce on impact."
%
%   freezing_rain → glaze_of_ice
%     https://www.weather.gov/iwx/sleetvsfreezingrain
%     (NOAA National Weather Service — U.S. government)
%     "...they instantly refreeze upon contact with anything that that is at or
%      below freezing (32 degrees F), creating a glaze of ice."
%
%   hail → balls_of_ice
%     https://forecast.weather.gov/glossary.php?word=HAIL
%     (NOAA National Weather Service Glossary — U.S. government)
%     "Showery precipitation in the form of irregular pellets or balls of ice
%      more than 5 mm in diameter, falling from a cumulonimbus cloud."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the NWS Glossary statement that
% fixes the first row (rain → drops). Every OTHER row's per-fact source and
% verbatim span is listed above, so each form remains independently checkable.
% All sources are the NOAA National Weather Service, a primary U.S. government
% source, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table precip_form {
    columns precip, form

    row (rain, drops)
    row (snow, ice_crystals)
    row (sleet, frozen_raindrops)
    row (freezing_rain, glaze_of_ice)
    row (hail, balls_of_ice)

    source "Precipitation that falls to earth in drops more than 0.5 mm in diameter."
    locator "https://forecast.weather.gov/glossary.php?word=RAIN"
    trust authoritative
}
